        "id": 5633
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-10-01T23:39:41Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Just so you are aware, there is a third algorithm for drawing paths via inner triangulation. This is the method we will need to use when the path is large and simple enough.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 206,
        "startChar": 14,
        "endLine": 206,
        "endChar": 32
      },
      "revId": "56de3e15bb27e2162c46e69a5ca6330103c7a0a6",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "d88096f3_5f4c31ce",
        "filename": "experimental/graphite/src/Device.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 206,
      "author": {
        "id": 8341
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-10-03T15:02:35Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I was planning on having that distinction handled internally in the DrawList-\u003eDrawPass conversion. stencilAndFillPath() is separate from fillConvexPath() because it can enforce requiring a separate stencil ordering index, which fillConvexPath doesn\u0027t require at all.\n\nIn terms of using that stencil ordering index, either a single pass of stencil with wedges, vs. two passes of stencil\u0027ed curves and stencil\u0027ed inner triangles, the stencil ordering constraint should be the same (the two stencil passes for a given path need to happen before the cover step, and need to not conflict with other path draws, but interactions between the 2 stencil passes is fine, right?).",
      "parentUuid": "e8be2511_365ceecd",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 206,
        "startChar": 14,
        "endLine": 206,
        "endChar": 32
      },
      "revId": "56de3e15bb27e2162c46e69a5ca6330103c7a0a6",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "55c68897_25f93fef",
        "filename": "experimental/graphite/src/Device.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 206,
      "author": {
        "id": 5633
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-10-04T17:58:51Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The \"inner triangulation\" algorithm is different. It\u0027s 3 passes:\n\n1) Stencil the curves (and breadcrumb triangles)\n\n2) Triangulate the inner fan (not with middle out triangulation, but using GrTriangulator) and draw those triangles directly to the framebuffer. (See https://github.com/google/skia/blob/main/src/gpu/ops/PathInnerTriangulateOp.cpp#L280 )\n\n3) Draw the curves (by drawing convex hulls around their control points) and breadcrumb triangles\n\nThat being said, \"stencil then cover\" is still a decent name for this algorithm, and we can still batch paths that don\u0027t overlap when using inner triangulation, so we could always decide further down the line whether to use wedges, middle out triangles and curves, OR \"inner triangulation\"",
      "parentUuid": "d88096f3_5f4c31ce",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 206,
        "startChar": 14,
        "endLine": 206,
        "endChar": 32
      },
      "revId": "56de3e15bb27e2162c46e69a5ca6330103c7a0a6",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
